it's great to welcome to the program  today Ian Haney Lopez who is a  constitutional law professor at the  University of California Berkeley and  also author of the book merged left  fusing race and class winning elections  and saving America it's so great to have  you on today thank you it's really great  to join you so the the angle I'm very  interested in is that of really thinking  about how to win because one of the  areas where I've been a bit disaffected  lately with my own movement the  progressive movement in which you know I  consider myself a member is that  sometimes I think we may miss the forest  for the trees  when it comes to how to win and you talk  very interestingly about the right and  wrong way to deal with race in politics  and sometimes the arguments that are  made are I feel overly simplistic like  identity politics is bad or good versus  actually a discussion of what we mean by  that and how something as important to  identity as race should be discussed so  maybe to start lay out the panorama as  you see it of how some of these issues  are being discussed in politics and how  you would maybe want to see that changed  so I think the number one thing to start  with there is the idea that race is  being used as a wedge issue to divide  Americans and more particularly to  divide a Democratic constituency that  used to include the white working-class  african-americans and white liberals  everybody understands that race is being  used as a wedge issue but the left today  is badly divided in how to respond the  more centrist position though this is a  position that's also adopted by economic  populist like Bernie Sanders is to focus  on winning over white voters and to  understand or to believe that talking  about race will alienate white voters  and so to a duck as a racial strategy  the tactic of not talking about race at  all so all the sort of amongst interest  Democrats and among some some radical  economic populist there's a tendency to  go colorblind to emphasize economic  issues and to downplay the centrality of  race that's on one side on the other  side especially among racial justice  activists you have a very strong sense  that we need to talk about race we need  to talk about it both in terms of the  systemic violence by government against  communities of color violence that's  evident in mass incarceration and over  policing and mass deportation ice  enforcement but also in the consistent  neglect of communities of color so we  need to address it and we need to  address as well frontally and head-on  the use of race as as a wedge issue in  politics that's the that's the divide  both camps are thinking about how to  deal with race and they're coming up  with very different solutions ignore it  and hope you can change the conversation  or attack it head-on so there's one I'll  call it a proposed solution that I read  about frequently and I want to see  whether you think it it really is sort  of up to the challenge of dealing with  this issue in a productive way which is  talk about race but always make it a  race class narrative like that's that's  the prescription that's how you do it  you talk about class understanding that  race is part of it you meld them  together you don't alienate anybody does  that shake how do you how would you  analyze that idea so so that's the idea  that I sort of come to a number of years  ago and worked on with unions and then  ultimately put together a research  project the race class narrative project  using communication specialists and  pollsters and and here's the core idea  and I and I really want to make this  point as shortly as I can this is not a  typical race plus class we should do  both type move this is a paradigm change  in how we think about the relationship  between race and class it's a paradigm  change because what it's saying is race  and class  are not in the United States separate  they're completely dependent on each  other or to put that more concretely in  the class war that the rich are winning  their number-one weapon is racial  division the rich are siphoning more and  more wealth to themselves because  they've been successful in dividing us  along race lines and this has very clear  implications if you want to if you want  economic populism and if you want racial  justice because what it says is if you  want economic populism you have to fight  race as a do as a tactic for dividing  American voters and what it says is if  you want racial justice you have to  fight racial division you have to make  it clear to rights that they have their  own pragmatic interest in opposing  racial division so that rights will  understand that they have their own  pragmatic interest in supporting racial  justice I'm reminded of the sort of  status quo that exists in much of the  agricultural world that deals with  immigration where we've seen large  corporations that benefit from both  documented and undocumented immigrant  labor very successfully convinced a lot  of Americans that this is really about  our brown people stealing your jobs and  that seems to me to have been a very  effective way of taking the attention  off of the fact that these corporations  want to pay the lowest possible wages  even if they are not fair wages that  these corporations disproportionately  take advantage with very little  consequence of undocumented immigrant  labor because those rules are often  ignored by the government as priorities  is that an example of how this is being  weaponized in a sense as you described  you know it's a really great example  because it illustrates two distinct  phenomenon we have to keep both in mind  one way to think about the connections  between race and class is to think about  the way in which economic exploitation  of people of color has been the  foundation for this country so you can  think about it as the racialization of  Native Americans as inferior as an  excuse to take land or the racialization  of people from Africa as black as an  excuse for slavery and so on through  manifest destiny and through today's  exploitation of immigrant labor and  especially undocumented immigrant labor  this is a story that says race and class  focus on what's happening to communities  of color in the way they're economically  exploited a very very important story  but here's the point don't stop there  the race and class are fused throughout  the American economy and as importantly  throughout the American government which  regulates the economy and this is the  other part of the story that you  surfaced not only are corporations that  explored exploiting brown and black and  Asian people but they're also turning  around and saying to White's the cause  of economic misery in your life are  these undeserving threatening others  blame them hate government because  government supposedly cares more about  them than it does about you you're on  your own and a buy a gun and B trust the  marketplace because through hard work  you'll take care of yourself and if you  fail you alone are responsible and what  this means is that the entire economy  the entire government the way government  is captured by the rich is also a  function of racial narratives in our  political sphere in our culture this is  incredibly important racing class is not  just a story about what's happening to  communities of color it's a story about  what's happening to the United States as  a whole it's a story about what has  happened to us over the last 50 years  we've seen the greatest transfer of  wealth upward from all of us into the  economic stratosphere that we've seen in  a century  why because many Americans working  to vote their racial fears you cannot  understand surging economic inequality  that impoverishes almost all of us  including whites without focusing on the  way in which race has been used as the  principal weapon against all of us yes  so that gets to trump in 2016 and I  should be interested in hearing your  thoughts about how you know in the  post-mortems of what happened there have  been all sorts of different analyses  that purport to know what it was that  allowed Donald Trump to win now  one merely mathematical point is when  you're talking about about 70,000 votes  in three states that could have flipped  it we want to be careful about pointing  to any one thing because there's  probably 30 different things that could  have made a seventy thousand vote  difference but what I want to focus in  on with you is the fear of losing status  that was in the minds of many voters who  did ultimately vote for Trump how does  that tie into the race class analysis  Trump wins overwhelmingly because of his  success and appealing to racial  resentment and racial anxiety but we  should be clear Trump was not the  originator of that strategy that's a  fifty-year-old strategy Republicans have  been trying that since 1964 right and  especially since 1970 it has been their  principal route to building popular  support to winning elections so when we  look at Mitt Romney's numbers 90 percent  of Mitt Romney support came from white  voters same with with Donald Trump 3 out  of 5 rights voted for Mitt Romney saying  with Donald Trump ninety-eight percent  of Republican elected officials are  white similar numbers under Trump Donald  Trump pushes more aggressively a  strategy that has been in place and has  been working for the Republicans for 50  years not since 1964 has a Democratic  candidate for president won a majority  of the white vote this is very  very much about race but it's also  important to pause and say that doesn't  mean this isn't about class there's art  of it  this debate is a trace or is a class for  many people afraid of losing status they  have lost status they have lost position  their lives are in peril many whites are  in trouble they're under tremendous  stress right this is the sort of  research that talks now about deaths of  despair suicide violence drug addiction  like most of us many whites are under  tremendous pressure and what's happening  is that the Republicans have created  that pressure by siphoning wealth up  into the up to the very rich but they've  also turned around and said you know why  your life is in such danger it's because  of people of color they're coming in  they're flooding the country they're  taking what they don't deserve and  they're also violent and they don't  respect life the way we do right  this is a race story to provide a frame  to interpret economic hardship I want to  be very clear economic hardship is not  the main thing here many people like  Robert Rice start by saying economic  hardship Verte first then people look  for someone to blame that's not the  history dog whistling starts and is  tremendously successful at a time in  1970 1972 when most working-class whites  were doing remarkably well race has its  own independent power but race and class  combined in a vicious circle the more  people vote their racial fears the more  they elect politicians who serve the  plutocrats the more those politicians  turn around and rig the system to help  very rich and beggar the rest of us the  more they feel stressed and look for  someone to blame and are encouraged to  use race as the focus of their hatred  and their anxiety it becomes a vicious  circle and no one better embodies that  than Donald Trump the last thing I want  to touch on and we have sort of limited  time left is I want to get your thoughts  on the identity politics debate that it  is sort of existing on the Left I'll  give you my sort of twenty second  position which is I am against the use  of identity as a cudgel as a basis on  which to silence someone as a basis on  which to disqualify somebody etc but I  do believe that one's identity gives  insight into what it is like to  experience the world through that  identity and so we should want that we  should welcome that and we should listen  to it that's where I kind of land on it  I don't know if that is a coherent  strategy for electoral politics however  oh I think it is and I would just extend  it further and I would say it's always  identity politics more than anything  else political scientists and democratic  realists understand people are voting in  terms of a series of questions Who am I  in this society who are my friends who  threatens me who can I work with those  are questions of identity and we are all  thinking in those terms so the problem  with the term like identity politics is  when it's applied to some groups but not  to others we should all be thinking that  all of us are operating organizing  voting in terms of identity and what  that means is we need to create the sort  of identity that includes an awful lot  of us a super majority of us because  although with a bigger we only with an  inclusive identity that takes identity  seriously can we actually create the  wave election that we need to change the  direction in this country and this is  where that race class fusion becomes so  important because what it says is all of  us want to take care of our families all  of us want a world in which we're  welcome and respected and safe but some  people are trying to divide us let's  come together over those lines of  division let's come together  specifically across racial lines because  when we come together and understand  we're all in this together  then and only then can we build a  political power to really change this  country's direction and that's going to  be good for all our families whether  were white black or brown  everybody's helped when we realize all  of our fates are linked very very  interesting we've been speaking with Ian  Haney Lopez who is a constitutional law  professor the book merged left fusing  race and class winning elections and  saving America thank you so much for  talking to me today I really appreciate  your time thanks so much I really  appreciate the conversation

